            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} cohesive (∞,1)-topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Smooth%E2%88%9EGrpd"} Smooth∞Grpd
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+groupoid"} ∞-Lie groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+an+%E2%88%9E-bundle"} ∞-connections
            on
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+%E2%88%9E-bundle"} principal ∞-bundle
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+group"} ∞-Lie group
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Chern-Weil+theory"} ∞-Chern-Weil theory
            deals with these higher connections and their relation to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+differential+cohomology"} ordinary differential cohomology
          %p Here we describe some introdutcory basics of the general theory in concrete terms.
          %p
            See
            %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/infinity-Chern-Weil+theory#Motivation"} ∞-Chern-Weil theory – motivation
            for some motivation.
          %p
            Two simplifying special cases of general
            = succeed "-Chern-Weil" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                restricting attention to
                %em low categorical degree
                ,
              %p
                studying
                = succeed "s," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal 1-bundle
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+2-bundle"} principal 2-bundle
                = succeed "s," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-groupoid"} 2-groupoid
                = succeed "s;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-groupoid"} 3-groupoid
            %li
              %p
                restricting attention to
                %em infinitesimal aspects
              %p
                studying not
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+groupoid"} ∞-Lie groupoid
                = succeed "s." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebroid"} ∞-Lie algebroid
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo =
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = \infty
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomological
                effects (very similar to how
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+homotopy+theory"} rational homotopy theory
                describes just non-torsion phenomena of genuine
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
          %p These are the special cases that this introduction concentrates on.
          %p We start by describing
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#PrincipalNBundles"} Smooth principal n-bundles
          %p
            for low
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            in detail, connecting them to standard theory, but presenting everything in such a way as to allow straightforward generalization to the full discussion of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+%E2%88%9E-bundle"} principal ∞-bundle
          %p Then in the same spirit we discuss
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#LowDimension"} Connections on principal n-bundles
          %p
            for low
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            in a fashion that connects to the ordinary notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parallel+transport"} parallel transport
            and points the way to the fully-fledged formulation in terms of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/path+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} path ∞-groupoid
            functor.
          %p This leads to differential-form expressions that we shall then finally reformulate in terms of
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#LieConnections"}> L-∞ algebra valued connections
              \.
          %p
            We end by indicating how under
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            this lifts to the full
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Chern-Weil+theory"} ∞-Chern-Weil theory
          %h2#PrincipalNBundles
            Principal
            = succeed "-bundles" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            We assume here that the reader has a working knowledge of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-groupoid"} 2-groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
            in terms of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complexes"} Kan complexes
          %h3#PrincipalBundles Ordinary smooth principal bundles
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            (all our smooth manifolds are assumed to be finite dimensional and
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/paracompact+space"} paracompact
          %p
            We give a discussion of smooth
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            in a manner that paves the way to a straightforward generalization to a description of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+%E2%88%9E-bundle"} principal ∞-bundle
          %p
            From the group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            we canonically obtain a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            that we write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}G
            and call the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
            groupoid of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo →
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{B}G = (G \stackrel{\to}{\to} *)
          %p
            with composition induced from the product in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                  %mo =
                  %mrow
                    %mo {
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo •
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mrow
                              %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi g
                                    %mn 1
                            %mo ↗
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo ↘
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi g
                                    %mn 2
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo •
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi g
                                  %mn 2
                                %mo ⋅
                                %msub
                                  %mi g
                                  %mn 1
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo •
                    %mo }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{B}G =
                  \\left\{
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; \bullet
                  \\\
                  \&amp; {}^{\mathllap{g_1}}\nearrow
                  \&amp;=&amp;
                  \\searrow^{\mathrlap{g_2}}
                  \\\
                  \\bullet &amp;&amp;\stackrel{g_2 \cdot g_1 }{\to}&amp;&amp; \bullet
                  }
                  \\right\}
          %p
            where the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_i \in G
            are elements in the group, and the bottom morphism is labeled by forming the product in the group. (The order of the factors here is a convention whose choice, once and for all, does not matter up to equivalence.)
          %p
            But we get a bit more, even. Since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}G
            that makes it a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoid"} Lie groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+groupoid"} internal groupoid
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diff"} Diff
            of
            = succeed "s:" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi LieGrpd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{B}G \in LieGrpd
          %p
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}G
            regarded as equipped with this smooth structure. Here and in the following the boldface is to indicate that we have an object equipped with a bit more structure – here: smooth structure – than present on the object denoted by the same symbols, but without the boldface. Eventually we will make this precise by having the boldface symbols denote objects in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Smooth%E2%88%9EGrpd"} Smooth∞Grpd
            which are taken by
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forgetful+functor"} forgetful functor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9EGrpd"} ∞Grpd
            denoted by the corresponding non-boldface symbols.
            %sup#fnref:1
              %a{:href => "#fn:1", :rel => "footnote"} 1
          %p
            Also the smooth manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            may be regarded as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoid"} Lie groupoid
            – a groupoid with only identity morphisms. Its cartoon description is simply
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi id
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  X = \{x \stackrel{id}{\to} x \}
                  \\,.
          %p
            But there are other groupoids associated with
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{U_i \to X\}_{i \in I}
            be an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+cover"} open cover
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cech+groupoid"} Cech groupoid
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(\{U_i\})
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ,
                        %mi j
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %mo ∩
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi j
                    %mover
                      %munder
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi p
                            %mn 2
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi p
                            %mn 1
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  C(\{U_i\})
                  \=
                  \\left(
                  \\coprod_{i,j} U_i \cap U_j
                  \\stackrel{\overset{p_1}{\to}}{\underset{p_2}{\to}}
                  \\coprod_i U_i
                  \\right)
                  \\,.
          %p A useful cartoon description of this groupoid is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mrow
                    %mo {
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo ,
                            %mi j
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↗
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↘
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo ,
                            %mi i
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo ,
                            %mi k
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo }
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  C(\{U_i\})
                  \=
                  \\left\{
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; (x,j)
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \nearrow &amp;=&amp; \searrow
                  \\\
                  (x,i) &amp;&amp;\to&amp;&amp; (x,k)
                  }
                  \\right\}
                  \\,.
          %p
            This indicates that the objects of this groupoid are pairs
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x,i)
            consisting of a point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
            and a patch
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i \subset X
            that contains
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x,i,j)
            consisting of a point and
            %em two
            patches, that both contain the point, in that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %mo ∩
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in U_i \cap U_j
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i
            are smooth manifolds and the inclusions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i \to X
            are
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(U)
            becomes a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoid"} Lie groupoid
          %p
            There is a canonical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi x
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  C(\{U_i\}) \to X \;\; :\;\; (x,i) \mapsto x
                  \\,.
          %p
            This functor is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+functor"} internal functor
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diff"} Diff
            and moreover it is evidently
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+surjective+functor"} essentially surjective
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+and+faithful+functor"} full and faithful
          %p
            However, while essential surjectivity and full-and-faithfulness implies that the underlying bare functor has a homotopy-inverse, that homotopy-inverse never has itself smooth component maps, unless
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            itself is a Cartesian space and the chosen cover is trivial.
          %p
            We do however want to think of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(\{U_i\})
            as being equivalent to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            even as a Lie groupoid. One says that a smooth functor whose underlying bare functor is an equivalence of groupoids is a
            %em weak equivalence
            of Lie groupoids, which we write as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ≃
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(\{U_i\}) \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(U)
            as a
            %em good
            equivalent replacement of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            if it comes from a cover that is in fact a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/good+open+cover"} good open cover
            in that all its non-empty finite intersections
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi i
                        %mn 0
                      %mi ⋯
                      %msub
                        %mi i
                        %mi k
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi i
                        %mn 0
                  %mo ∩
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ∩
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi i
                        %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_{i_0 \cdots i_k} := U_{i_0} \cap \cdots \cap U_{i_k}
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphic"} diffeomorphic
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mi dim
                        %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{dim X}
          %p
            We shall discuss later in which precise sense this condition makes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(U)
            %em good
            in the sense that smooth functors out of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(U)
            model the correct notion of morphism out of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            in the context of smooth groupoids (namely it will mean that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(U)
            is cofibrant in a suitable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            structure on the category of Lie groupoids). The formalization of this statement is what
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            theory is all about, to which we will come. For the moment we shall be content with accepting this as an ad hoc statement.
          %p
            Observe that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  g : C(U) \to \mathbf{B}G
          %p is given in components precisely by a collection of functions
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %mo →
                  %mi G
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ,
                      %mi j
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\{g_{i j} : U_{i j} \to G \}_{i,j \in I}
          %p
            such that on each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∩
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi k
                  %mo ∩
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i \cap U_k \cap U_j
            the equality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mi k
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_{j k} g_{i j}  = g_{i k}
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo ,
                            %mi j
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↗
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↘
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo ,
                            %mi i
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo ,
                            %mi k
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo )
                  %mo ↦
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo •
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mrow
                              %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi g
                                    %mrow
                                      %mi i
                                      %mi j
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                  %mi x
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ↗
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo ↘
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi g
                                    %mrow
                                      %mi j
                                      %mi k
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                  %mi x
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo •
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi g
                                  %mrow
                                    %mi i
                                    %mi k
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi x
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo •
                    %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\left(
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; (x,j)
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \nearrow &amp;&amp; \searrow
                  \\\
                  (x,i) &amp;&amp;\to&amp;&amp; (x,k)
                  }
                  \\right)
                  \\mapsto
                  \\left(
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; \bullet
                  \\\
                  \&amp; {}^{\mathllap{g_{i j}(x)}}\nearrow
                  \&amp;&amp; \searrow^{\mathrlap{g_{j k}(x)}}
                  \\\
                  \\bullet &amp;&amp;\stackrel{g_{i k}(x)}{\to}&amp;&amp; \bullet
                  }
                  \\right)
                  \\,.
          %p
            It is well known that such collections of functions characterize
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal bundle
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "-bundles" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            First observe that in total we have discussed so far
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/span"} span
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi C
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi U
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi g
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi G
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mo ≃
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  C(U) &amp;\stackrel{g}{\to}&amp; \mathbf{B}G
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow^{\mathrlap{\simeq}}
                  \\\
                  X
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            Such spans of functors, whose left leg is a weak equivalence, are sometimes known, essentially equivalently, as
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morita+morphism"} Morita morphism
            %em generalized morphisms
            of Lie groupoids, as
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilsum-Skandalis+morphism"} Hilsum-Skandalis morphism
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bibundle"} bibundle
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anafunctor"} anafunctor
            %em models
            for more intrinsically defined direct morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to \mathbf{B}G
            in the
            = succeed "-topos" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "-Lie" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            Now consider yet another Lie groupoid canonically associated with
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi E
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}G
            for the groupoid whose formal description is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi E
                  %mi G
                  %mo =
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mi G
                    %mo ×
                    %mi G
                    %mover
                      %munder
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi p
                            %mn 1
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mo ⋅
                    %mi G
                    %mo )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{E}G  =
                  \\left(
                  G \times G \stackrel{\overset{\cdot}{\to}}{\underset{p_1}{\to}} G
                  \\right)
          %p with the evident composition operation. The cartoon description of this groupoid is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi E
                  %mi G
                  %mo =
                  %mrow
                    %mo {
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi g
                              %mn 2
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mrow
                              %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi g
                                    %mn 2
                                  %msubsup
                                    %mi g
                                    %mn 1
                                    %mrow
                                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                                      %mn 1
                            %mo ↗
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo ↘
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi g
                                    %mn 3
                                  %msubsup
                                    %mi g
                                    %mn 2
                                    %mrow
                                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                                      %mn 1
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi g
                              %mn 1
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi g
                                  %mn 3
                                %msubsup
                                  %mi g
                                  %mn 1
                                  %mrow
                                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                                    %mn 1
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi g
                              %mn 3
                    %mo }
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{E}G
                  \=
                  \\left\{
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; g_2
                  \\\
                  \&amp; {}^{\mathllap{g_2 g_1^{-1}}}\nearrow &amp;=&amp; \searrow^{\mathrlap{g_3 g_2^{-1}}}
                  \\\
                  g_1 &amp;&amp;\stackrel{ g_3 g_1^{-1}}{\to}&amp;&amp; g_3
                  }
                  \\right\}
                  \\,,
          %p
            This again inherits an evident smooth structure from the smooth structure of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            and hence becomes a Lie groupoid.
          %p
            There is an evident
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forgetful+functor"} forgetful functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi E
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{E}G \to \mathbf{B}G
          %p which sends
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↦
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo •
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi g
                        %mn 2
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mn 1
                  %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
